    {
      "id": "adr-issues",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Debt is tracked as issues labelled tech-debt",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 0,
      "concern": "technical-debt",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code lives in this element and every deadline leaves a shortcut in it; whether those shortcuts are seen, counted and repaid decides how fast the element can change next year. The forces: debt taken knowingly for a deadline is a sound loan and debt taken unknowingly is a surprise, and both charge interest in every change that touches them (Cunningham's metaphor, Fowler's quadrant); a debt nobody can see is never repaid, while a register nobody prunes becomes a graveyard; time for repayment competes with features in every planning, and a team that gets none slows down without anyone having decided it; deprecated code, endpoints and feature flags stay because removing them has no sponsor and no date; and a full rewrite looks like freedom from all debt and usually recreates it, two years late (Spolsky). What a change must pass to be merged is decided by the Code quality gate concern and which tests it has by the Test strategy concern; this decision is about the debt that is already there and the debt taken on purpose.",
      "decision": "Every debt item is an issue in the tracker (GitHub Issues, Jira or Linear) with the label tech-debt, an assignee as owner, a cost estimate in days and a Consequence section; an issue form (.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/tech-debt.yml with required fields) makes cost and consequence mandatory, and the weekly triage closes or completes an item without an assignee. Debt is triaged in the same weekly triage as bugs. Code points at an item with TODO (KEY-123): lint fails a TODO without a key and a CI script fails the build on a TODO whose issue is closed. Repayment budget: {{BUDGET}}. Deprecated code, endpoints and flags: {{DEPRECATION}}. Rewrites: {{REWRITE}}. Age: {{AGE}}. A shortcut taken knowingly in a pull request (a waived quality-gate condition, a suppressed warning, a skipped test) opens its issue in the same pull request.",
      "consequences": "• Debt competes for the same backlog as features and bugs, on the same board, with the same estimates.\n• The share of debt work per iteration falls out of the tracker for free.\n• A tracker fills with small items quickly; triage must close duplicates and merge siblings or the label becomes noise.\n• Architectural debt gets a ticket-sized description; the reasoning behind it needs an ADR beside the issue.",
      "alternatives": "1. Decision records in the repository: better for architectural debt with reasoning, but invisible to the planning board unless mirrored.\n2. A code-analysis platform as the register: free for debt in the code itself, blind to debt in design, process or missing tests.\n3. A wiki page or spreadsheet: easy to start, outside every workflow, and the first thing to go stale.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REGISTRY": [
          "issues"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-adrs",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Debt is recorded as decision records",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 130,
      "concern": "technical-debt",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code lives in this element and every deadline leaves a shortcut in it; whether those shortcuts are seen, counted and repaid decides how fast the element can change next year. The forces: debt taken knowingly for a deadline is a sound loan and debt taken unknowingly is a surprise, and both charge interest in every change that touches them (Cunningham's metaphor, Fowler's quadrant); a debt nobody can see is never repaid, while a register nobody prunes becomes a graveyard; time for repayment competes with features in every planning, and a team that gets none slows down without anyone having decided it; deprecated code, endpoints and feature flags stay because removing them has no sponsor and no date; and a full rewrite looks like freedom from all debt and usually recreates it, two years late (Spolsky). What a change must pass to be merged is decided by the Code quality gate concern and which tests it has by the Test strategy concern; this decision is about the debt that is already there and the debt taken on purpose.",
      "decision": "Debt is recorded as decision records in the repository, docs/debt/NNNN-title.md in Nygard's format (context, decision, consequences) with front matter owner, cost_days, status and, where one exists, sunset; managed with adr-tools or log4brains and reviewed in the pull request like code. Its number is its key: TODO (DEBT-0042) points at it from the code, lint fails a TODO without a key, and a test over the records fails the build on a missing owner, cost or consequences section or a sunset in the past. Items too small for a record are TODO comments pointing at the record of the debt they belong to. Repayment budget: {{BUDGET}}. Deprecated code, endpoints and flags: {{DEPRECATION}}. Rewrites: {{REWRITE}}. Age: {{AGE}}. A shortcut taken knowingly in a pull request adds or amends a record in the same pull request.",
      "consequences": "• The reasoning travels with the code: a record says why the debt was taken and what repaying it means, which a ticket never does.\n• Records are reviewed, versioned and searchable with the code; no second system.\n• Planning does not see docs/debt unless the team mirrors items into the tracker or reads the register at every planning.\n• Small items are expensive to record, so some go unrecorded; the TODO rule catches those that reach the code.",
      "alternatives": "1. Issues labelled tech-debt: visible in planning, cheaper per item, poorer at reasoning.\n2. A code-analysis platform as the register: measures debt in the code, says nothing about why it is there.\n3. Debt sections inside the architecture ADRs themselves: fewer files, but the debt disappears into decisions nobody reopens.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REGISTRY": [
          "adrs"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-tool",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Debt is tracked in the code-analysis platform",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 260,
      "concern": "technical-debt",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code lives in this element and every deadline leaves a shortcut in it; whether those shortcuts are seen, counted and repaid decides how fast the element can change next year. The forces: debt taken knowingly for a deadline is a sound loan and debt taken unknowingly is a surprise, and both charge interest in every change that touches them (Cunningham's metaphor, Fowler's quadrant); a debt nobody can see is never repaid, while a register nobody prunes becomes a graveyard; time for repayment competes with features in every planning, and a team that gets none slows down without anyone having decided it; deprecated code, endpoints and feature flags stay because removing them has no sponsor and no date; and a full rewrite looks like freedom from all debt and usually recreates it, two years late (Spolsky). What a change must pass to be merged is decided by the Code quality gate concern and which tests it has by the Test strategy concern; this decision is about the debt that is already there and the debt taken on purpose.",
      "decision": "SonarQube (or SonarCloud) is the register: each open issue carries its remediation effort, the sum is the technical debt, and the debt ratio (remediation effort over development cost, SQALE) is a quality-gate condition, at most {{MAX_DEBT_RATIO_PCT}}% on new code; the project dashboard shows the overall ratio and effort and is the report. Every open issue is assigned to an owner; an issue is accepted (won't fix) only with a comment giving the reason, and accepted issues are the tolerated debt. TODO comments are flagged by rule S1135 and counted as debt. There is no separate list; debt outside the code (a design to change, a missing test suite) gets a ticket or ADR only by exception and is linked from a Sonar issue where one exists. Repayment budget: {{BUDGET}}. Deprecated code, endpoints and flags: {{DEPRECATION}}. Rewrites: {{REWRITE}}. Age: {{AGE}}.",
      "consequences": "• Debt in the code is measured without anyone keeping a list, with one number everyone understands.\n• The gate stops new debt from entering while the overall ratio is worked down (Clean as You Code).\n• Design, process and test debt are not code smells; the platform does not see them, so they go unrecorded unless someone makes an exception.\n• Remediation effort is the rule's estimate, not the team's; the ratio is a trend to watch, not a budget to spend.",
      "alternatives": "1. Issues labelled tech-debt: sees every kind of debt, but each item is written by hand.\n2. Decision records in the repository: best for architectural debt, worst for the hundreds of small smells the platform finds.\n3. Both the platform and a tracker: complete, until the two disagree and neither is maintained.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REGISTRY": [
          "tool"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-strangler",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "Rewrites follow the strangler fig",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 390,
      "concern": "technical-debt",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code lives in this element and every deadline leaves a shortcut in it; whether those shortcuts are seen, counted and repaid decides how fast the element can change next year. The forces: debt taken knowingly for a deadline is a sound loan and debt taken unknowingly is a surprise, and both charge interest in every change that touches them (Cunningham's metaphor, Fowler's quadrant); a debt nobody can see is never repaid, while a register nobody prunes becomes a graveyard; time for repayment competes with features in every planning, and a team that gets none slows down without anyone having decided it; deprecated code, endpoints and feature flags stay because removing them has no sponsor and no date; and a full rewrite looks like freedom from all debt and usually recreates it, two years late (Spolsky). What a change must pass to be merged is decided by the Code quality gate concern and which tests it has by the Test strategy concern; this decision is about the debt that is already there and the debt taken on purpose. Here the element must keep serving while any part of it is rewritten.",
      "decision": "A rewrite replaces one piece at a time behind a facade: a reverse proxy, API gateway or an in-process routing module that sends each piece's calls to the old or the new implementation (Envoy, NGINX, Spring Cloud Gateway or a feature flag per piece in the code). Old and new run side by side; each piece has a cut-over, a comparison period (shadow traffic or a diff of both answers where the piece allows it) and a way back by routing. New code reaches the old module only through the facade; a dependency rule fails the build on a direct import of src/legacy. A piece is done when the old implementation is deleted. Big-bang rewrites are not done; a proposal for one is an ADR that supersedes this decision.",
      "consequences": "• Delivery continues during the rewrite and every piece is proven in production before the next starts.\n• A failed piece is routed back in minutes; nothing is bet on a single cut-over day.\n• The facade and two implementations cost running and reasoning effort until the last piece is gone; a strangler that stops halfway is the worst of both.\n• Pieces must be found along seams (a bounded context, a route, a table); an element without seams needs them cut first.",
      "alternatives": "1. A full rewrite with a written case: one cut-over, months without delivery, and the old code's undocumented behaviour rediscovered in production.\n2. Refactoring in place without a facade: cheaper when the technology stays; not a rewrite.\n3. Running the new system for new customers only: a strangler by tenant, viable when data need not move.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REWRITE": [
          "strangler"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "adr-big-bang",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "A full rewrite is allowed with an accepted case",
      "x": 780,
      "y": 520,
      "concern": "technical-debt",
      "status": "accepted",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Code lives in this element and every deadline leaves a shortcut in it; whether those shortcuts are seen, counted and repaid decides how fast the element can change next year. The forces: debt taken knowingly for a deadline is a sound loan and debt taken unknowingly is a surprise, and both charge interest in every change that touches them (Cunningham's metaphor, Fowler's quadrant); a debt nobody can see is never repaid, while a register nobody prunes becomes a graveyard; time for repayment competes with features in every planning, and a team that gets none slows down without anyone having decided it; deprecated code, endpoints and feature flags stay because removing them has no sponsor and no date; and a full rewrite looks like freedom from all debt and usually recreates it, two years late (Spolsky). What a change must pass to be merged is decided by the Code quality gate concern and which tests it has by the Test strategy concern; this decision is about the debt that is already there and the debt taken on purpose. Here the element is small or leaving a platform, so a full rewrite is on the table.",
      "decision": "A full rewrite of the element or of a module may be started only after a case written as an ADR is accepted in writing by the owners: cost in person-months, risks, parity criteria (the tests and behaviours the new code must match, run against both), what the old code handles that no document describes, a data migration and a cut-over plan with a rollback. Until the ADR is accepted no rewrite code is merged. Where the element serves users during the rewrite, the strangler-fig approach is preferred and the case must say why it does not apply.",
      "consequences": "• A rewrite becomes a decision with a price instead of a mood; most proposals do not survive writing the case.\n• When one is accepted, the parity criteria make \"done\" testable.\n• The old system still has to be maintained while the new one is built; the case has to budget for both.\n• Months of a rewrite are months without visible features; stakeholders accept that in the ADR, not after.",
      "alternatives": "1. Strangler fig only: no rewrite stops delivery, but a small element or one leaving its platform pays for a facade it does not need.\n2. No rule: rewrites start on enthusiasm and end on budget.\n3. Rewrite only within fixed windows: bounds the cost but rarely the risk.",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REWRITE": [
          "big-bang-allowed"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-todo-key",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Every TODO names a key",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 0,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 TODO, FIXME, HACK or XXX comments without a register key of the form KEY-123",
      "description": "A script run in lint greps the tracked source files for TODO-like comments and fails on any that does not carry a key in the agreed form, with or without a sunset in brackets before it: TODO (KEY-123): text, or TODO [2027-01-31] (KEY-123): text. A semgrep pattern-regex rule does the same inside semgrep ci.\n\nTool: git grep in a lint step (or semgrep pattern-regex)\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# scripts/check-todos.sh: every TODO/FIXME names a register key, e.g. TODO (SHOP-123): ...\nset -euo pipefail\nbad=$(git grep -nE '\\b(TODO|FIXME|HACK|XXX)\\b' -- ':!node_modules' ':!*.md' ':!scripts/check-todos.sh' \\\n  | grep -vE '\\b(TODO|FIXME|HACK|XXX)\\b( *\\[[^]]+\\])? *\\(([A-Z]+-[0-9]+)\\)' || true)\nif [ -n \"$bad\" ]; then\n  printf 'TODO without a register key (use TODO (KEY-123): text):\\n%s\\n' \"$bad\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REGISTRY": [
          "issues",
          "adrs"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-todo-closed",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "No TODO points at a closed issue",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 130,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 TODO comments whose issue is closed; the build fails on the first",
      "description": "A CI step collects every key from TODO comments, asks the tracker for the issue's state and fails when one is closed: the debt was repaid or dropped and the comment is stale. The snippet reads GitHub issues with gh (key SHOP-123 is issue 123 of the repository); for Jira replace the gh call with GET /rest/api/3/issue/KEY?fields=status.\n\nTool: gh api in a CI step\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# scripts/check-todo-issues.sh: a TODO (SHOP-123) must point at an open issue\nset -euo pipefail\nREPO=${GITHUB_REPOSITORY:-acme/shop}\nstale=0\nfor key in $(git grep -ohE '\\b(TODO|FIXME)\\b[^(]*\\(([A-Z]+-[0-9]+)\\)' -- ':!node_modules' | grep -oE '[A-Z]+-[0-9]+' | sort -u); do\n  state=$(gh api \"repos/$REPO/issues/${key##*-}\" --jq .state 2>/dev/null || echo missing)\n  if [ \"$state\" != \"open\" ]; then echo \"TODO ($key): issue is $state\"; stale=1; fi\ndone\nexit $stale\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REGISTRY": [
          "issues"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-debt-register",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Debt records are complete and not past sunset",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 260,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 records in docs/debt without owner, cost_days and a Consequences section; 0 open records with a sunset in the past",
      "description": "A test in the unit suite parses the front matter of every record in docs/debt and fails on a missing owner, a cost of 0 or none, a missing Consequences heading, or a record not marked repaid whose sunset date has passed.\n\nTool: Vitest with gray-matter\n\n```ts\nimport { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport matter from 'gray-matter'\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\n\nconst files = readdirSync('docs/debt').filter((f) => /^\\d{4}-.*\\.md$/.test(f))\n\nit.each(files)('%s has an owner, a cost, consequences and a live sunset', (file) => {\n  const { data, content } = matter(readFileSync(`docs/debt/${file}`, 'utf8'))\n  expect(data.owner, 'owner').toBeTruthy()\n  expect(Number(data.cost_days), 'cost_days').toBeGreaterThan(0)\n  expect(content).toMatch(/^## Consequences/m)\n  if (data.status !== 'repaid' && data.sunset) expect(Date.parse(data.sunset)).toBeGreaterThan(Date.now())\n})\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REGISTRY": [
          "adrs"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-debt-ratio",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Debt ratio in the quality gate",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 390,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "Technical debt ratio on new code at most {{MAX_DEBT_RATIO_PCT}}% and maintainability rating A on new code; the quality gate fails the pull request otherwise",
      "description": "The project's quality gate carries the conditions new_sqale_debt_ratio > {{MAX_DEBT_RATIO_PCT}} and new_maintainability_rating > A as errors, added once through the API (or in the gate's settings page), and the pipeline waits for the gate with sonar.qualitygate.wait=true so that a failed gate fails the build.\n\nTool: SonarQube quality gate (api/qualitygates)\n\n```bash\n# once, for the gate the project uses (SonarQube 10.x; SonarCloud: the same API under sonarcloud.io)\ncurl -sf -u \"$SONAR_TOKEN:\" -X POST \"$SONAR_URL/api/qualitygates/create_condition\" \\\n  -d gateName=Shop -d metric=new_sqale_debt_ratio -d op=GT -d error=5        # MAX_DEBT_RATIO_PCT\ncurl -sf -u \"$SONAR_TOKEN:\" -X POST \"$SONAR_URL/api/qualitygates/create_condition\" \\\n  -d gateName=Shop -d metric=new_maintainability_rating -d op=GT -d error=1  # A on new code\n\n# sonar-project.properties, so that the pipeline fails when the gate does\n# sonar.newCode.referenceBranch=main\n# sonar.qualitygate.wait=true\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REGISTRY": [
          "tool"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-debt-share",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Repayment share reported",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 520,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "The share of closed work items labelled tech-debt is reported every iteration; with a fixed share, it is at least {{DEBT_SHARE_PCT}}%",
      "description": "A scheduled job at the end of each iteration counts the work items closed since the iteration started and those among them labelled tech-debt, posts the share with the number of open items, and, where a fixed share is agreed, fails below it so that the retrospective sees it. With Jira, the two counts come from JQL: resolved >= startOfSprint() and labels = tech-debt.\n\nTool: gh issue list in a scheduled job (or a Jira JQL filter)\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# scripts/debt-share.sh [iteration-start YYYY-MM-DD]; DEBT_SHARE_PCT set = fail below it\nset -euo pipefail\nSINCE=${1:-$(date -d '14 days ago' +%F 2>/dev/null || date -v-14d +%F)}\nall=$(gh issue list --state closed --search \"closed:>=$SINCE\" --limit 500 --json number --jq length)\ndebt=$(gh issue list --state closed --label tech-debt --search \"closed:>=$SINCE\" --limit 500 --json number --jq length)\nopen=$(gh issue list --state open --label tech-debt --limit 500 --json number --jq length)\nshare=$(( debt * 100 / (all > 0 ? all : 1) ))\necho \"since $SINCE: $debt of $all closed items were debt ($share%), $open debt items open\"\n[ -z \"${DEBT_SHARE_PCT:-}\" ] || [ \"$share\" -ge \"$DEBT_SHARE_PCT\" ] || { echo \"below the agreed $DEBT_SHARE_PCT%\"; exit 1; }\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-expiring-todo",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Sunsets expire in the build",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 650,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 TODO comments past their date; a TODO with a sunset less than {{SUNSET_GRACE_DAYS}} days ahead at the time it is added is refused in review",
      "description": "Deprecated code carries its sunset as a dated TODO beside the @deprecated marker, TODO [2027-01-31] (KEY-123): remove, and unicorn/expiring-todo-comments turns the comment into a lint error the day after the date. On the JVM the same is done with a @Deprecated(forRemoval = true) marker and a test over an annotation processor's list; in Python with a dated warnings.deprecated message checked by a grep in CI.\n\nTool: ESLint with eslint-plugin-unicorn (expiring-todo-comments)\n\n```js\n// eslint.config.js\nimport unicorn from 'eslint-plugin-unicorn'\n\nexport default [{\n  plugins: { unicorn },\n  rules: {\n    'unicorn/expiring-todo-comments': ['error', {\n      allowWarningComments: false,      // a TODO without a condition is an error\n      ignoreDatesOnPullRequests: false, // the date fails on pull requests too\n      terms: ['todo', 'fixme'],\n    }],\n  },\n}]\n// in the code:\n// /** @deprecated use OrdersV2Client: v1 has no idempotency keys. TODO [2027-01-31] (SHOP-123): remove v1 */\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "DEPRECATION": [
          "sunset"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-flag-age",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "No feature flag outlives its sunset",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 780,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 release or experiment flags older than {{MAX_FLAG_AGE_DAYS}} days or past their retire-by date; permanent flags are marked permanent",
      "description": "Flags are declared in flags.json with owner, creation date, kind and a retire-by date; a test in the unit suite fails on a non-permanent flag older than {{MAX_FLAG_AGE_DAYS}} days or past its retire-by date. With LaunchDarkly or Unleash the same test reads the flag list from their API and uses the creation date there.\n\nTool: Vitest (a test over flags.json)\n\n```ts\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\nimport flags from '../flags.json' // [{ key, owner, kind: 'release' | 'experiment' | 'permanent', since: '2026-08-01', retireBy: '2026-10-30' }]\n\nconst MAX_DAYS = Number(process.env.MAX_FLAG_AGE_DAYS ?? 90)\nconst days = (iso: string): number => (Date.now() - Date.parse(iso)) / 86_400_000\n\nit('no release or experiment flag outlives its sunset', () => {\n  const stale = flags\n    .filter((f) => f.kind !== 'permanent')\n    .filter((f) => days(f.since) > MAX_DAYS || (f.retireBy && Date.parse(f.retireBy) < Date.now()))\n    .map((f) => `${f.key} (owner ${f.owner}, since ${f.since})`)\n  expect(stale).toEqual([])\n})\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-no-deprecated",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "No new calls to deprecated code",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 910,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 calls to code marked @deprecated without a line-level disable naming a register key",
      "description": "Lint fails on a call to or import of anything marked @deprecated, using type information so that re-exports and overloads count too; an existing call is tolerated only with a line disable whose comment names the register key. On the JVM the same is javac -Xlint:deprecation -Werror; in .NET, CS0618 as an error in the project file.\n\nTool: typescript-eslint (@typescript-eslint/no-deprecated)\n\n```js\n// eslint.config.js\nimport tseslint from 'typescript-eslint'\n\nexport default tseslint.config({\n  files: ['src/**/*.ts', 'src/**/*.tsx'],\n  languageOptions: { parserOptions: { projectService: true } },\n  rules: { '@typescript-eslint/no-deprecated': 'error' },\n})\n// a tolerated existing call names its debt item:\n// // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-deprecated -- SHOP-123, goes with the v1 sunset\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-strangler-boundary",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Legacy reached only through the facade",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1040,
      "category": "structural",
      "threshold": "0 imports of src/legacy from outside src/legacy and src/facade",
      "description": "A dependency rule fails the build when any module outside the legacy code and the facade imports the legacy code directly, so that new code grows only on the new side and the old module can be deleted piece by piece. On the JVM an ArchUnit rule noClasses().that().resideOutsideOfPackages(\"..legacy..\", \"..facade..\").should().dependOnClassesThat().resideInAPackage(\"..legacy..\") does the same.\n\nTool: dependency-cruiser\n\n```js\n// .dependency-cruiser.cjs\nmodule.exports = {\n  forbidden: [{\n    name: 'legacy-only-through-facade',\n    comment: 'ADR Strangler fig: new code calls the facade; the legacy module is reached only through it',\n    severity: 'error',\n    from: { pathNot: '^src/(legacy|facade)/' },\n    to: { path: '^src/legacy/' },\n  }],\n  options: { tsConfig: { fileName: 'tsconfig.json' } },\n}\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REWRITE": [
          "strangler"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-rewrite-case",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "A rewrite has an accepted case",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1170,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 pull requests labelled rewrite without an ADR of kind rewrite with status accepted; every such ADR names at least 2 accepting owners and the sections Cost, Risks, Parity criteria and Cut-over",
      "description": "Rewrite cases are ADRs with kind: rewrite in their front matter. A test in the unit suite fails on a rewrite ADR that is not accepted by at least two owners or lacks one of the required sections, and a pull-request step fails a pull request labelled rewrite whose description does not link an accepted rewrite ADR (gh pr view --json body, grep for docs/adr/).\n\nTool: Vitest with gray-matter (plus a gh pr view step)\n\n```ts\nimport { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs'\nimport matter from 'gray-matter'\nimport { expect, it } from 'vitest'\n\nconst SECTIONS = ['## Cost', '## Risks', '## Parity criteria', '## Cut-over']\nconst rewrites = readdirSync('docs/adr').filter((f) => f.endsWith('.md'))\n  .map((f) => ({ f, ...matter(readFileSync(`docs/adr/${f}`, 'utf8')) }))\n  .filter(({ data }) => data.kind === 'rewrite')\n\nit.each(rewrites)('$f is an accepted rewrite case', ({ data, content }) => {\n  expect(data.status).toBe('accepted')\n  expect(data.accepted_by?.length ?? 0, 'owners who accepted it').toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(2)\n  for (const s of SECTIONS) expect(content, s).toContain(s)\n})\n// pull request labelled rewrite: gh pr view \"$PR\" --json body --jq .body | grep -q 'docs/adr/' || exit 1\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "REWRITE": [
          "big-bang-allowed"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-boy-scout",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Touched files do not get worse",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1300,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "In every pull request the lint warnings of the files it modifies are at most what they were on the base branch",
      "description": "A pull-request step lints the modified files on the head and, in a temporary worktree, on the base branch, and fails when the warning count grew; new files are covered by the quality gate's own conditions. With SonarQube the same is a gate condition on new code; this script needs no platform.\n\nTool: ESLint in a pull-request step (git worktree)\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# scripts/warnings-do-not-grow.sh: lint warnings in modified files, head vs base\nset -euo pipefail\nBASE=${BASE_REF:-origin/main}\nfiles=$(git diff --name-only --diff-filter=M \"$BASE...HEAD\" -- '*.ts' '*.tsx')\n[ -z \"$files\" ] && exit 0\ncount() { npx eslint --no-error-on-unmatched-pattern --format json $files | jq '[.[].warningCount] | add // 0'; }\nafter=$(count)\ngit worktree add -q \"$RUNNER_TEMP/base\" \"$BASE\"\nbefore=$(cd \"$RUNNER_TEMP/base\" && npm ci --silent && count)\ngit worktree remove --force \"$RUNNER_TEMP/base\"\necho \"warnings in touched files: $before -> $after\"\n[ \"$after\" -le \"$before\" ] || { echo 'touched files got worse; leave them at least as clean'; exit 1; }\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "BUDGET": [
          "boy-scout"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff-debt-age",
      "type": "fitness-fn",
      "label": "Old debt items are escalated",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 1430,
      "category": "process",
      "threshold": "0 debt items older than {{MAX_DEBT_AGE_DAYS}} days without the label escalated or accepted-debt",
      "description": "A weekly job lists open debt items older than {{MAX_DEBT_AGE_DAYS}} days that carry neither escalated nor accepted-debt, posts them to the owners' leads and fails so that the run shows red until each is scheduled or closed as accepted. With records in the repository the same job reads the date in the front matter; with SonarQube, api/issues/search with createdBefore and statuses=OPEN.\n\nTool: gh issue list in a scheduled job\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# scripts/debt-age.sh: weekly; MAX_DEBT_AGE_DAYS defaults to 180\nset -euo pipefail\nexport MAX=${MAX_DEBT_AGE_DAYS:-180}\nold=$(gh issue list --label tech-debt --state open --limit 500 --json number,title,createdAt,labels --jq '\n  .[] | select((now - (.createdAt | fromdateiso8601)) > (env.MAX | tonumber) * 86400)\n      | select(any(.labels[].name; . == \"escalated\" or . == \"accepted-debt\") | not)\n      | \"#\\(.number) \\(.title) (opened \\(.createdAt[:10]))\"')\nif [ -n \"$old\" ]; then\n  printf 'debt items older than %s days without a decision:\\n%s\\n' \"$MAX\" \"$old\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n```",
      "hubWhen": {
        "AGE": [
          "max-age"
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
